  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single red start pixel on the left edge",
  "path": "start horizontally; each ray paints until the first original colored marker in each direction, then emits the perpendicular row or column from the cell just before that marker",
  "trajectory": "paint traversed background pixels red while preserving all original markers",
  "multi_step": "process emitted rows/columns depth-first; each marker opens at most once, later hits only stop, and a lower-edge orange marker hit from above is terminal"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 4-12 rows x 6-12 cols, black background
  start: single red pixel on left edge (col 0), row in upper 22-43% of grid
  markers:
    colors: subset of {green(3), pink(6), orange(7), teal(8)}, 1-4 distinct colors
    count: 1-19 total single pixels on black background
    no marker shares col 0 with start (but other markers may be at col 0) [observed]
    at least one marker in the start row to the right of col 0 (ensures initial turn)
  ray_network:
    initial_axis: horizontal from start pixel
    rule: ray paints bg cells red until hitting a marker; first hit on a marker opens it (emits perpendicular ray from cell before marker); subsequent hits only stop
    terminal: orange marker on bottom row hit from above stops without opening
    traversal: depth-first (stack), right/down before left/up
  path_complexity:
    depth: 1-6 turns (median ~3)
    total_segments: 2-11
    markers_opened: 1-10 (remaining markers are distractors/unreached)
    red_coverage: 20-42% of grid cells
  output: input with all traversed bg cells painted red; markers preserved in place

generation_strategy:
  approach: path-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H x W in observed ranges
    2. Place start red pixel at (r, 0) where r is in row range [H//5, H//2-1]
    3. Build the ray network iteratively starting horizontal from start pixel
       - pick a target depth 1-6; at each segment, place a marker along the ray's path
         at a random distance (>=1 cell gap from origin), choosing a random color from {3,6,7,8}
       - the cell before the marker becomes the origin of the next perpendicular segment
       - alternate h/v axis; optionally branch (place markers in both directions)
       - optionally place one orange marker on the bottom row reachable by a downward vertical ray for terminal stop
    4. Scatter 0-12 distractor markers (same color palette) on cells not on any ray path,
       ensuring they wont be hit by extending rays (not in line-of-sight of any active segment)
    5. Verify solver produces >=1 turn and 20-42% red coverage; retry if degenerate
```
